  133: ## 2. Acquisition, pinning and verification at build time
  134: 
  135: ### 2.1 Where this runs
  136: 
  137: `.github/workflows/release.yml`'s `build-macos` job runs on **`ubuntu-latest`**
  138: (release.yml:252) ƒ?" there is no macOS runner in the workflow at all. The arm64
  139: payload it consumes is produced upstream in `build-linux` (also `ubuntu-latest`)
  140: by `runtime/pack-native.sh --arch arm64` (release.yml:192-198).
  141: 
  142: So Debian tooling is available. It is nonetheless **not used**.
  143: 
  144: ### 2.2 Decision: direct pinned HTTPS fetch + sha256 lockfile, not `apt-get download`
  145: 
  146: `apt-get download qemu-user` on the builder resolves against *whatever
  147: `deb.debian.org/dists/trixie` currently serves*. That is a moving target: trixie
  148: point releases and security updates change `libc6`, `libssl3t64` and `qemu`
  149: versions without notice, and the builder is Ubuntu, so it would additionally
  150: need a trixie `sources.list`, `dpkg --add-architecture arm64`, and an `apt`
  151: sandbox ƒ?" machinery whose only output is "some `.deb` files".
  152: 
  153: Instead: **fetch exact pool URLs over HTTPS and verify each against a committed
  154: sha256 lockfile.** No apt, no dpkg, no containers, no foreign-arch setup on the
  155: builder. Every byte that ships is named in git.
  156: 
  157: The lockfile is `packaging/macos/guest-assets/qemu-user.lock` ƒ?" one row per
  158: package:
  159: 
  160: ```
  161: # package | version | arch | sha256 | pool path (relative to a Debian archive root)
  162: qemu-user|1:10.0.11+ds-0+deb13u1|arm64|bb194fdc...|pool/main/q/qemu/qemu-user_10.0.11+ds-0+deb13u1_arm64.deb
  163: ```
  164: 
  165: Fetch order per row, first success wins, **sha256 decides in every case**:
  166: 
  167: 1. `https://deb.debian.org/debian/<pool path>` ƒ?" fast, CDN-backed, but Debian
  168:    *removes* superseded pool files when a package is updated.
  169: 2. `https://snapshot.debian.org/archive/debian/<TIMESTAMP>/<pool path>` ƒ?" the
  170:    permanent archive, immune to supersession, but slow and rate-limited.
  171: 
  172: This is deliberately belt-and-braces and it answers the reproducibility
  173: question directly: **the lockfile, not the mirror, is the pin.** A mirror that
  174: serves the wrong bytes fails the hash and the build stops. `deb.debian.org` is
  175: the fast path; `snapshot.debian.org` is what keeps the build reproducible after
  176: the pool rotates. Both were verified live (A7.3).
  177: 
  178: Verified during design: all 11 pool URLs return 200 on `deb.debian.org` today,
  179: and `snapshot.debian.org` serves both the pool path and the `/mr/binary/` API
  180: for these exact versions.
  181: 
  182: ### 2.3 Where the fetch lives
  183: 
  184: New script **`packaging/macos/guest-assets/fetch-qemu-user.sh`**, invoked from
  185: `runtime/pack-native.sh` only when a new opt-in flag is passed:
  186: 
  187: ```
  188: pack-native.sh --arch arm64 --bundle-guest-qemu
  189: ```
  190: 
  191: The flag is **opt-in and off by default** on purpose. `pack-native.sh` also
  192: produces the generic "any systemd glibc Linux server" package; a plain arm64
  193: server install has no business carrying 67 MiB of x86_64 translation for a
  194: Lima guest. Only the macOS release path passes it (release.yml:192-198).
  195: 
  196: Fail-closed: if the flag is passed and any row cannot be fetched or fails its
  197: hash, `pack-native.sh` exits non-zero. There is no "carry on without the
  198: bundle" branch ƒ?" that is precisely the silent-degradation mode this change
  199: exists to remove.
