  201: ---
  202: 
  203: ## 3. GPL compliance
  204: 
  205: Both packages are GPL. `qemu-user` is QEMU: GPL-2.0 with components under
  206: LGPL-2.1 and other compatible licenses. `binfmt-support` is GPL-3.0+. Once
  207: iolbox ships the binaries, iolbox owes the obligations ƒ?" this is the whole point
  208: of the owner's decision.
  209: 
  210: The Windows QEMU section of `THIRD_PARTY.md` is the house precedent: pin an
  211: exact version + checksum, document how the redistributable was produced,
  212: document the shipped layout, include the license texts in the shipped directory,
  213: and record a written offer for source pointing at the corresponding source for
  214: that exact version. This plan **matches or exceeds** it on each point.
  215: 
  216: ### 3.1 License text ƒ?" Debian's own `copyright` files, not a generic GPL
  217: 
  218: Verified by extraction (A7.1), Debian packages carry their canonical
  219: copyright/license file at `/usr/share/doc/<package>/copyright`, in machine-
  220: readable DEP-5 format. There is no top-level `COPYING`. So the Windows recipe's
  221: "include `COPYING`/`COPYING.LIB`" translates to "include the `copyright` files".
  222: 
  223: These are *better* than a generic GPL text: `qemu-user-static`'s copyright file
  224: is 31,253 bytes of per-file license attribution across the whole QEMU tree,
  225: including the `Files-Excluded` list recording what Debian stripped from
  226: upstream. A generic `COPYING` would lose all of that. Using it is not a
  227: shortcut ƒ?" it is the correct artifact.
  228: 
  229: Two independent copies ship, deliberately:
  230: 
  231: 1. **Inside each `.deb`**, untouched ƒ?" automatic, since the packages ship
  232:    unmodified.
  233: 2. **At the archive top level**, extracted and committed to the repo, so a user
  234:    can read the license without extracting a payload and then a `.deb`:
  235: 
  236: ```
  237: iolbox-macos-arm64/notices/qemu-user.copyright        (31,253 bytes)
  238: iolbox-macos-arm64/notices/binfmt-support.copyright   (967 bytes)
  239: ```
  240: 
  241: **Provenance is verifiable.** The M7 record captured sha256s of these exact
  242: files as installed on the qualification Mac. Both reproduce byte-for-byte from
  243: the pool today (A7.1):
  244: 
  245: | File | sha256 | M7 record |
  246: |---|---|---|
  247: | `qemu-user-static/copyright` | `7092076611fd6b8499ae39720ed86b7977a5dfa378038966d85d9987f7cfe784` | matches |
  248: | `binfmt-support/copyright` | `6b3d59446db277b4c13b9acc08cb1a24b7dcceaa7cd1ca8522720ccbaa40665e` | matches |
  249: 
  250: That is a strong result: the bytes this plan proposes to ship are provably the
  251: same bytes that were legally reviewed during M7 qualification.
  252: 
  253: Note `qemu-user`, `qemu-user-binfmt` and `qemu-user-static` are all built from
  254: the **`qemu`** source package and share one copyright file; shipping it once
  255: under the name `qemu-user.copyright` covers all three.
  256: 
  257: ### 3.2 Written offer for corresponding source
  258: 
  259: Recorded in `THIRD_PARTY.md` and carried in the archive at
  260: `notices/qemu-user.copyright`'s side as part of the notices section.
  261: 
  262: For **QEMU** (`qemu-user`, `qemu-user-binfmt`, `qemu-user-static`), source
  263: package `qemu`, version `1:10.0.11+ds-0+deb13u1`:
  264: 
  265: - `https://deb.debian.org/debian/pool/main/q/qemu/qemu_10.0.11+ds-0+deb13u1.dsc`
  266: - `https://deb.debian.org/debian/pool/main/q/qemu/qemu_10.0.11+ds.orig.tar.xz`
  267: - `https://deb.debian.org/debian/pool/main/q/qemu/qemu_10.0.11+ds-0+deb13u1.debian.tar.xz`
  268: - Browsable: `https://sources.debian.org/src/qemu/1%3A10.0.11%2Bds-0%2Bdeb13u1/`
  269: - Equivalent: `apt-get source qemu-user-static` on a trixie guest with
  270:   `deb-src` enabled.
  271: 
  272: For **binfmt-support**, binary version `2.2.2-7+b1`:
  273: 
  274: - `https://deb.debian.org/debian/pool/main/b/binfmt-support/binfmt-support_2.2.2-7.dsc`
  275: - `https://deb.debian.org/debian/pool/main/b/binfmt-support/binfmt-support_2.2.2.orig.tar.gz`
  276: - `https://deb.debian.org/debian/pool/main/b/binfmt-support/binfmt-support_2.2.2-7.debian.tar.xz`
  277: 
  278: **The `+b1` is deliberate and must not be "corrected".** `2.2.2-7+b1` is a
  279: binNMU ƒ?" a binary-only rebuild by Debian's buildds against updated build-deps,
  280: with **no source change**. The corresponding source for binary `2.2.2-7+b1` is
  281: source `2.2.2-7`. There is no `binfmt-support_2.2.2-7+b1.dsc` and asking for one
  282: returns 404. All six source URLs above were verified to return 200 (A7.3).
  283: 
  284: Because iolbox ships these `.deb` files **bit-for-bit unmodified**, the offer
  285: correctly points at Debian upstream ƒ?" exactly the reasoning the Windows section
  286: already uses ("iolbox distributes QEMU **unmodified**; the source offer
  287: therefore points at upstream"). A1.3's decision not to trim is what keeps this
  288: sentence true.
  289: 
  290: ### 3.3 Open legal question ƒ?" flagged, not answered
  291: 
  292: **GPL-3.0 A6 / GPL-2.0 A3 permit satisfying the source obligation either by
  293: accompanying the binaries with source, or by a written offer valid for three
  294: years. A URL pointing at a third party's archive (Debian) is the weaker of the
  295: available options, and its sufficiency is a legal judgment, not an engineering
  296: one.** GPL-2.0 A3(c) explicitly permits passing along a received offer only for
  297: noncommercial distribution.
  298: 
  299: This plan reproduces the posture `THIRD_PARTY.md` already takes for the Windows
  300: QEMU build, so it is at least *internally consistent* and does not make iolbox's
  301: position worse. But it does not independently establish that a
  302: pointer-to-Debian offer is sufficient for iolbox's distribution.
  303: 
  304: The conservative alternative ƒ?" mirroring the ~50 MB of `qemu` source tarballs
  305: into the release, or hosting them on a URL iolbox controls ƒ?" is mechanically
  306: easy and is *not* proposed here only because it is a legal call above this
  307: document's pay grade.
  308: 
  309: **This is stated as an open question for the owner, not resolved.** See A8.
  310: 
  311: ---
  312: 
  313: ## 4. Guest-side install, offline
  314: 
  315: ### 4.1 Delivery: inside the arm64 payload tarball
  316: 
  317: Three routes exist for getting `.deb` files into the guest. Chosen route and
  318: reasoning:
  319: 
  320: | Route | Verdict |
  321: |---|---|
  322: | **(a)** New rows in `release-manifest.txt` | **Rejected.** `pack-release.sh:139` and `release-layout-test.sh:171` both require every manifest source to be a regular non-symlink file *inside the repo* ƒ?" so this mandates committing 67 MiB of binary `.deb` blobs into git, permanently, per version bump. |
  323: | **(b)** Extra `limactl copy` + widened flatten glob | **Rejected.** `macos_lima.go:415` flattens only `*.sh` out of the staged guest dir. Widening it means Go launcher changes and a new copy/mv pair ƒ?" more moving parts for no benefit. |
  324: | **(c)** Carried inside the arm64 payload tarball | **Chosen.** |
  325: 
  326: Route (c) requires **no Go launcher change, no `release-manifest.txt` change to
  327: carry the `.deb`s, and no change to any of the five hardcoded archive-layout
  328: assertions** for the `.deb`s themselves. The payload tarball is already:
  329: 
  330: - built by `pack-native.sh --arch arm64`,
  331: - sha256-verified end-to-end through CI (`release.yml:289-329`, then
  332:   `pack-release.sh:118-121`),
  333: - covered by the archive's internal `SHA256SUMS`,
  334: - copied to the guest by `limactl copy`.
  335: 
  336: The `.deb`s inherit every one of those guarantees for free.
  337: 
  338: **Ordering ƒ?" the one thing that makes this work.** Provisioning order is
  339: `10-multiarch-native.sh` ƒ+' `20-kernel-hold` ƒ+' `30-canary` ƒ+'
  340: `40-install-payload-native.sh` ƒ+' `50-verify` (`macos_lifecycle.go:312`). Step 10
  341: runs *long before* the payload-install step. But staging happens **once, before
  342: the whole sequence** (`macos_lifecycle.go:388`), and the env map ƒ?" including
  343: `IOLBOX_PAYLOAD_TARBALL=/opt/iolbox-provision/payload/<basename>`
  344: (`macos_lifecycle.go:222`) ƒ?" is applied uniformly to **every** step
  345: (`macos_lifecycle.go:202-222`).
  346: 
  347: So `10-multiarch-native.sh` can read `$IOLBOX_PAYLOAD_TARBALL` and extract just
  348: the `guest-assets/qemu-user/` subtree from it. The tarball is on the guest and
  349: addressable at step 10. **This was the load-bearing uncertainty in the design
  350: and it resolves cleanly.**
  351: 
  352: Payload layout addition:
  353: 
  354: ```
  355: iolbox-server-<version>-linux-arm64/
  356:   guest-assets/
  357:     qemu-user/
  358:       arm64/*.deb          (5 files)
  359:       amd64/*.deb          (6 files)
  360:       qemu-user.copyright
  361:       binfmt-support.copyright
  362:       MANIFEST             (package|version|arch|sha256|filename, mirrors the lockfile)
  363:       SOURCE-OFFER.txt     (A3.2, verbatim)
  364: ```
  365: 
  366: ### 4.2 The `10-multiarch-native.sh` change
  367: 
  368: `run_provision()` currently does: `dpkg --add-architecture amd64` ƒ+'
  369: `apt-get update` ƒ+' `apt-get install ... ` ƒ+' `update-binfmts --enable`.
  370: 
  371: It becomes: `dpkg --add-architecture amd64` ƒ+' **extract bundle ƒ+' verify each
  372: `.deb` sha256 against the bundled `MANIFEST` ƒ+' `dpkg -i` the whole set** ƒ+'
  373: `update-binfmts --enable`.
  374: 
  375: `dpkg --add-architecture amd64` is still required before installing any `:amd64`
  376: package and stays exactly as it is.
  377: 
  378: Notable properties:
  379: 
  380: - **`verify_runtime()` (lines 182-211) needs no change.** It asserts *end state*
  381:   ƒ?" `dpkg-query` install status, `dpkg --print-foreign-architectures`, the
  382:   binfmt handler at `/proc/sys/fs/binfmt_misc/qemu-x86_64`, and real ELF
  383:   `e_machine` bytes via `assert_amd64_elf`. All of that is equally true after
  384:   `dpkg -i`. This is a good sign the original script was written at the right
  385:   altitude.
  386: - Bundling `qemu-user-static` (the transitional package) even though nothing
  387:   needs it functionally is a **deliberate 70 KB spend**: it keeps
  388:   `package_is_installed_native qemu-user-static` (line 185) true, preserving
  389:   continuity with the M7 evidence and the pinned version on record in P7-02.
  390: - `IOLBOX_EXIT_APT` (4) remains the correct failure code; only its doc comment
  391:   widens from "apt / repository failure" to also cover local dpkg installs.
  392: - `dpkg -i` is order-insensitive when given all packages in one invocation ƒ?"
  393:   it unpacks all, then configures all ƒ?" so no manual topological ordering is
  394:   needed. The set being dependency-complete (A1.1) is what makes this safe.
  395: 
  396: ### 4.3 No silent network fallback ƒ?" enforced, not just intended
  397: 
  398: The offline motivation is defeated entirely if the script quietly falls back to
  399: apt when the bundle is missing or broken. So:
  400: 
  401: - **No `apt-get update` and no `apt-get install` remain in the native
  402:   provisioning path at all.** Not as a fallback, not behind a conditional. The
  403:   commands are deleted, not guarded.
  404: - Missing bundle, failed extraction, sha256 mismatch, or `dpkg -i` failure all
  405:   `die "$IOLBOX_EXIT_APT"` immediately.
  406: - `packaging/macos/tests/source-policy-test.sh` gains an assertion that
  407:   `10-multiarch-native.sh` contains no `apt-get install` / `apt-get update`
  408:   invocation, so a future edit cannot reintroduce the network dependency
  409:   without tripping a test.
  410: 
  411: That last item is what turns "we intend this to be offline" into something
  412: mechanically checked.
  413: 
  414: ### 4.4 Honest scope of the offline claim
  415: 
  416: Bundling makes **package installation** offline. It does **not** make the
  417: `native-arm64` profile offline end-to-end: Lima still downloads the digest-locked
  418: Debian cloud image on first run (`iolbox-native-arm64.yaml:24-27`). The archive
  419: is not an air-gapped install.
  420: 
  421: The accurate claim is: *once the guest image is present, native-arm64
  422: provisioning no longer depends on Debian's mirrors, and no longer breaks when a
  423: trixie point release moves `libc6`.* `THIRD_PARTY.md` and the release notes must
  424: say that and not overclaim. **Removing the trixie-moves-under-us failure mode is
  425: arguably the bigger practical win for a learner tool than offline per se** ƒ?" the
  426: current path is one Debian point release away from an unreproducible guest.
  427: 
  428: ---
  429: 
  430: ## 5. `THIRD_PARTY.md` changes
  431: 
  432: 1. **A"Separately installed and fetched components"** ƒ?" the bullet saying guest
  433:    packages "are fetched at provisioning time and are not embedded in the
  434:    archive" gains an explicit carve-out for the bundled translator set.
  435: 2. **A"QEMU and the Apple Silicon archive"** ƒ?" retitled and rewritten. The
  436:    headline "**No QEMU binary is contained in `iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz`**"
  437:    is now false and is replaced. The three "Boundary, stated explicitly"
  438:    bullets ƒ?" in particular "iolbox does **not** redistribute `qemu-user-static`
  439:    or `binfmt-support`" and "No QEMU or binfmt-support binary, source, or
  440:    copyright file is embedded in any iolbox artifact for macOS" ƒ?" are replaced
  441:    with the affirmative description: what ships, at which pinned versions and
  442:    checksums, in which layout, produced how.
  443: 3. **New "License / GPL compliance" subsection**, mirroring the Windows section's
  444:    shape: license texts included (A3.1), written offer for source (A3.2),
  445:    copyright attribution, and the explicit statement that the `.deb` files are
  446:    redistributed unmodified.
  447: 4. **The "Open prerequisite, owner action required" block is resolved and
  448:    closed.** It is replaced with a short record of what actually happened: the
  449:    review contemplated by `redistribution_review_required: true` was performed,
  450:    and its outcome was a decision to redistribute deliberately with full
  451:    compliance rather than to rely on the provisioning-time boundary. That block
  452:    asked for the question to be answered before the first tag shipping
  453:    `native-arm64`; it has now been answered, and the answer is recorded here and
  454:    in `docs/macos-m7-result.md`.
  455: 5. The Windows QEMU section is **not** touched.
  456: 
  457: `docs/macos-m7-result.md`'s P7-02 row gets a back-annotation pointing at this
  458: document, so the ledger stops reporting an open obligation that is now closed.
  459: 
  460: ---
  461: 
  462: ## 6. Version bump and changelog
  463: 
  464: **Yes ƒ?" this warrants a version bump and a changelog entry**, on three grounds:
  465: 
  466: - Archive contents change materially (+67 MiB, new `notices/` files, new payload
  467:   subtree).
  468: - Redistribution posture changes, which is legally significant and must be
  469:   discoverable from release notes, not just from a doc diff.
  470: - Provisioning behavior changes: a guest that previously required Debian mirror
  471:   reachability at step 10 no longer does.
  472: 
  473: Concretely: a **minor** bump, a `CHANGELOG.md` entry, and a release-notes line in
  474: `release.yml` replacing the current "installed from Debian at provisioning time"
  475: wording (release.yml:448), which becomes false.
  476: 
  477: The release notes must also carry the written offer for source (A3.2), matching
  478: what the Windows section already instructs ("Include this URL in the release
  479: notes / About page alongside the version above so the offer travels with the
  480: binary").
  481: 
  482: ---
  483: 
  484: ## 7. What was verified during design, and how
  485: 
  486: All of the following was run against the live Debian archive from the
  487: development host. No claim in A1-A3 is assumed.
  488: 
  489: ### 7.1 The packages were downloaded and extracted
  490: 
  491: `qemu-user-static_10.0.11+ds-0+deb13u1_arm64.deb` and
  492: `binfmt-support_2.2.2-7+b1_arm64.deb` were fetched from `deb.debian.org` and
  493: unpacked with a hand-written `ar` + `tar.xz` reader (no `dpkg` on this host).
  494: 
  495: - Confirmed `qemu-user-static`'s `control` declares `Section: oldlibs` and a
  496:   transitional description, and its `data.tar.xz` contains **88 entries, none of
  497:   which is an emulator binary** ƒ?" only symlinks, `qemu-debootstrap`, man pages
  498:   and docs. This is the A1 correction, established by extraction rather than
  499:   inference.
  500: - Confirmed `qemu-user-binfmt` contains only `/usr/lib/binfmt.d/qemu-*.conf`
  501:   symlinks, including `qemu-x86_64.conf`.
  502: - Extracted `/usr/share/doc/<pkg>/copyright` from both and computed sha256:
  503:   both **match the M7 record exactly** (A3.1 table). Both are DEP-5 format;
  504:   neither package contains a top-level `COPYING`, confirming the A3.1 approach.
  505: 
  506: ### 7.2 The dependency closure was computed from the real indices
  507: 
  508: `dists/trixie/main/binary-{arm64,amd64}/Packages.xz` were downloaded and parsed.
  509: This produced the version, size, sha256 and pool path for all 11 packages in
