## Confirmed sound

- The “big correction” is real: trixie’s `qemu-user-static` is a compatibility/transitional package depending on `qemu-user` and `qemu-user-binfmt`; the actual static emulators are in the roughly 64 MB `qemu-user` package. [Debian qemu-user-static metadata](https://packages.debian.org/trixie/arm64/qemu-user-static), [Debian qemu-user metadata](https://packages.debian.org/trixie/arm64/qemu-user)
- `binfmt-support 2.2.2-7+b1` on arm64 corresponds to source `2.2.2-7`; the `+b1` binNMU explanation is correct. [Debian binfmt-support metadata](https://packages.debian.org/trixie/binfmt-support)
- `$IOLBOX_PAYLOAD_TARBALL` is populated before step 10: staging completes at [macos_lifecycle.go:388](</J:/Claude code/iolab-qemu-redist-wt/tools/iolab-launcher/macos_lifecycle.go:388>), the path is placed in the shared environment at [macos_lifecycle.go:202](</J:/Claude code/iolab-qemu-redist-wt/tools/iolab-launcher/macos_lifecycle.go:202>), and step 10 is first in the sequence at [macos_lifecycle.go:309](</J:/Claude code/iolab-qemu-redist-wt/tools/iolab-launcher/macos_lifecycle.go:309>).
- Deleting both `apt-get update` and `apt-get install` from the native script removes the explicit repository fallback. No other named launcher path performs an apt fallback.
- Keeping the complete Debian QEMU packages unmodified is a defensible implementation choice; the flaw is in the incomplete dependency/compliance envelope around them, not that choice itself.
