Overall verdict: the central QEMU package correction is right, and the launcher delivery path is sound. The design is not yet shippable, however. It has two independent blockers: the offline package closure is incomplete/version-fragile, and the proposed compliance treatment covers only part of what the archive redistributes.

## BLOCKER

1. **The 11-package set is not dependency-complete.**

   [docs/macos-native-arm64-qemu-redistribution-plan.md:61](</J:/Claude code/iolab-qemu-redist-wt/docs/macos-native-arm64-qemu-redistribution-plan.md:61>) labels the set complete, but `libgcc-s1:amd64 14.2.0-19` has an exact dependency on `gcc-14-base (= 14.2.0-19)`, which is absent from the table. Debian’s package metadata confirms both that dependency and the source package involved. [Debian libgcc-s1 metadata](https://packages.debian.org/trixie/amd64/libgcc-s1)

   `gcc-14-base:arm64` cannot generally satisfy an unqualified dependency from `libgcc-s1:amd64`; an `amd64` instance is needed. This alone makes offline `dpkg -i` capable of leaving `libgcc-s1:amd64`, `libc6:amd64`, and everything above them unconfigured.

   The closure calculation described at [docs/...plan.md:506](</J:/Claude code/iolab-qemu-redist-wt/docs/macos-native-arm64-qemu-redistribution-plan.md:506>) therefore either stopped one level too soon or applied incorrect multiarch dependency semantics.

2. **The plan does not solve same-version co-installation for `Multi-Arch: same` packages.**

   Pinning `libc6:amd64` at `2.41-12+deb13u3` is safe only if the pinned guest already has `libc6:arm64` at exactly that version, or the transaction also supplies/upgrades the arm64 instance. Debian requires co-installed `Multi-Arch: same` instances to have identical versions. [Debian Policy §5.6.34.3](https://www.debian.org/doc/debian-policy/ch-controlfields.html), [Debian Handbook multiarch explanation](https://www.debian.org/doc/manuals/debian-handbook/sect.manipulating-packages-with-dpkg.en.html)

   The same audit is required for every bundled foreign-architecture `Multi-Arch: same` package that may already exist natively: at least `libgcc-s1`, `gcc-14-base`, `libssl3t64`, `libzstd1`, `zlib1g`, and possibly `openssl-provider-legacy`.

   The immutable image pin at [pinned-image-native-arm64.env:11](</J:/Claude code/iolab-qemu-redist-wt/packaging/macos/lima/pinned-image-native-arm64.env:11>) makes this solvable, but the document never inventories package versions inside that QCOW2. Reading only the current trixie index does not prove compatibility with the pinned image. A complete design must either:

   - prove and lock the native versions already in that exact QCOW2; or
   - bundle matching native packages and treat the operation as an offline coordinated upgrade.

   A second `dpkg --configure --pending` pass cannot repair a version mismatch.

3. **GPL and notice handling covers only QEMU and binfmt-support, while the archive redistributes every bundled `.deb`.**

   The bundle in [docs/...plan.md:68](</J:/Claude code/iolab-qemu-redist-wt/docs/macos-native-arm64-qemu-redistribution-plan.md:68>) includes packages from at least these source packages:

   - `qemu`
   - `binfmt-support`
   - `libpipeline`
   - `glibc`
   - `gcc-14`
   - `openssl`
   - `libzstd`
   - `zlib`

   Section 3 provides notices and source treatment only for the first two. The remaining packages include LGPL/GPL-covered components and other licenses with their own notice conditions. Shipping their `.deb`s makes iolbox their redistributor too; calling them “dependencies” does not remove those obligations.

   The top-level notice set at [docs/...plan.md:229](</J:/Claude code/iolab-qemu-redist-wt/docs/macos-native-arm64-qemu-redistribution-plan.md:229>) and source list at [docs/...plan.md:262](</J:/Claude code/iolab-qemu-redist-wt/docs/macos-native-arm64-qemu-redistribution-plan.md:262>) must cover every redistributed source package, including the missing `gcc-14-base` package once the closure is corrected.

4. **The proposed DEP-5 files are necessary attribution, but not sufficient standalone license copies.**

   The assertion at [docs/...plan.md:223](</J:/Claude code/iolab-qemu-redist-wt/docs/macos-native-arm64-qemu-redistribution-plan.md:223>) that the Debian copyright file replaces generic GPL texts is too strong. Debian copyright files conventionally refer to `/usr/share/common-licenses/GPL-2`, `GPL-3`, `LGPL-2.1`, etc. rather than embedding those texts. Debian Policy explicitly describes that arrangement. [Debian Policy §12.5](https://www.debian.org/doc/debian-policy/policy.pdf)

   That works as part of an installed Debian system because `base-files` supplies `/usr/share/common-licenses`. A macOS archive containing nested `.deb`s and two extracted DEP-5 files does not itself contain those referenced texts in a directly accessible form.

   Keep the DEP-5 files—they carry valuable per-file copyrights and exceptions—but also ship the complete license texts they reference. The same audit is needed for the copyright files of all other bundled packages.

5. **Section 3.3 leaves the central source-compliance mechanism unresolved while the document claims “full compliance.”**

   A URL to Debian is not, by itself, iolbox’s GPL written offer. GPLv2 §3(b) requires the distributor to offer the source to any third party for at least three years; §3(c)’s pass-through option is limited to occasional noncommercial redistribution where the distributor actually received such an offer. GPLv3 §6 has similarly specific offer/network-access alternatives. [GPLv2 §3](https://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/gpl-2.0.html), [GPLv3 §6](https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gpl.en.html)

   Therefore [docs/...plan.md:290](</J:/Claude code/iolab-qemu-redist-wt/docs/macos-native-arm64-qemu-redistribution-plan.md:290>) is not merely flagging an ancillary legal question; it defers a release-blocking implementation choice. The doc should choose a concrete compliant path before declaring P7-02 closed:

   - accompany the binary archive with corresponding source; or
   - provide equivalent source access under iolbox’s control from the same distribution point; or
   - make a genuine iolbox written offer, with an operational process and retention commitment.

   Matching the Windows wording does not establish sufficiency. In fact, the Windows precedent at [THIRD_PARTY.md:179](</J:/Claude code/iolab-qemu-redist-wt/THIRD_PARTY.md:179>) is itself weak: it points to a generic upstream QEMU source page, while the shipped build reports a post-release commit and the bundle is trimmed.

